<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Douglas Hyde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Dubhghlas de hÍde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Douglas Hyde was an Irish scholar of the Irish language and the first President of Ireland, from 1938 to 1945. In 1893 he co-founded the Gaelic League to revive the Irish language and culture, which inspired a generation of nationalists. A Protestant, he was chosen as an agreed candidate by the main parties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/douglas-hyde.jpg" alt="Douglas Hyde" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Douglas_Hyde,_circa_1940.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hyde was born on 17 January 1860 at Longford House in Castlerea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-roscommon">County Roscommon</a>. His father was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> rector, and Hyde grew up at Kilmactranny in Sligo and then at Frenchpark in Roscommon, taught at home by his father and aunt. As a boy he was fascinated by the Irish spoken by older people nearby, above all the gamekeeper Séamus Hart. He rejected family pressure to become a clergyman and studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a>, where he became fluent in French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, German, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and graduated in 1884.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In November 1892 Hyde wrote a manifesto, The necessity for de-anglicising the Irish nation, arguing that Ireland should follow its own traditions in language, literature and dress. In 1893 he helped found the Gaelic League, which promoted Irish culture, music, dance and language. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrick-pearse">Pádraig Pearse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamon-de-valera">Éamon de Valera</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-collins">Michael Collins</a> and Ernest Blythe all became politicised through it. Uncomfortable with the growing politicisation of the movement, he resigned its presidency in 1915. He was elected to the Seanad in 1925 and became Professor of Irish at University College Dublin.</p><p>In 1938 he was chosen after inter-party negotiations as the first President of Ireland, elected unopposed. Both main parties admired him, wanted to honour his work for the Irish language, and saw a non-Catholic president as an answer to the claim that the state was confessional. He was inaugurated on 26 June 1938 and recited the declaration of office in Irish. He kept to a quiet reading of the office and suffered a severe stroke in April 1940.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hyde left office on 25 June 1945 and did not seek a second term. He lived his last four years in a house on the grounds of Áras an Uachtaráin and died on 12 July 1949, aged 89. At his state funeral at St Patrick's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> service, all but one Catholic member of the cabinet waited outside, because of the rules of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> then in force. He was buried at Frenchpark.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Douglas Hyde” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Constitution of Ireland, drafted under Eamon de Valera and approved by referendum in 1937, replaced the Irish Free State with a state named Ireland, or Eire, with an elected President, <b>Douglas Hyde</b> being the first.</blockquote><a href="/state/ireland">Ireland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Castlerea, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.77,-8.5&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Castlerea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4361 317 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Douglas Hyde"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4811 587h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4811 587h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.77,-8.5&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
